 dev/cerebras-kimi/openclaw.json | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 54 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 dev/cerebras-kimi/README.md
 create mode 100644 dev/cerebras-kimi/openclaw.json

diff --git a/dev/cerebras-kimi/README.md b/dev/cerebras-kimi/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..3c620b55fb8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dev/cerebras-kimi/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+# Kimi on Cerebras: OpenClaw config
+
+`openclaw.json` here is an example config. It points OpenClaw's built-in Cerebras provider at Kimi on Cerebras's
+chat completions API (`https://api.cerebras.ai/v1`, model `moonshotai-kimi-k3.1-pro-trial`) and makes it the default
+model. Merge it into your OpenClaw config (`~/.openclaw/openclaw.json` by default) and set `CEREBRAS_API_KEY` in the
+Gateway's environment. Requests go straight from OpenClaw to Cerebras, with no proxy.
+
+The built-in Cerebras catalog does not list Kimi, so the model is declared here. The settings follow what this
+endpoint accepts:
+
+| Setting | Why |
+| --- | --- |
+| `api: openai-completions`, no `thinkingFormat` | OpenClaw's OpenAI-style defaults send `reasoning_effort`, and multi-step tool runs go through. Cerebras answers HTTP 400 to a `thinking` field and to `reasoning_content` on earlier assistant messages; the `deepseek` thinking format sends both. |
+| `supportedReasoningEfforts: ["low", "high", "max"]` | The efforts offered for this model (Kimi here also takes `medium`; `none`, `minimal` and `xhigh` get HTTP 400). The UI then offers off, minimal, low, medium, high, max and ultra, and OpenClaw sends the supported effort at or below the level: `minimal`, `low` and `medium` as `low`, `high` as `high`, `max` and `ultra` as `max`. With thinking off, no effort is sent and the server's default applies. |
+| `supportsStrictMode: false` | Strict tool schemas are rejected for this model on some accounts. |
+| `reasoning: true`, `input: ["text", "image"]` | Kimi streams its reasoning and accepts images (browser screenshots). |
+| `thinkingDefault: "low"` | Sessions start at low effort; change it in the UI or drop the line for OpenClaw's default. |
+
+The generation-speed display in the chat relies on Cerebras's `time_info`: every response's last streamed chunk
+carries `time_info.completion_time` and `usage.completion_tokens`, and the UI shows their ratio per model call as
+`<mean> tok/s mean · <p90> p90 (server)`.
